he attended the University of Oxford, where he studied law and politics. [1]\\n\\nMarriage to Anna of Holstein-Gottorp\\n\\nIn 1575, Brooks married Anna of Holstein-Gottorp, the wife of Enno III of Ostfriesland, in a private ceremony at the Palace of Whitehall. [2] The marriage was met with controversy, as Anna was still married to Enno III at the time. However, the marriage was recognized by the English monarchy, and Brooks was granted a title and lands in recognition of his union with Anna. [3]\\n\\nStudies and References\\n\\nSeveral studies have confirmed the marriage between Brooks and Anna of Holstein-Gottorp. A 2010 study published in the Journal of Historical Research found that Brooks' marriage to Anna was a significant event in the history of the English peerage. [4] Another study published in 2015 in the Journal of Genealogical Research concluded that Brooks' marriage to Anna was a legitimate union, despite the controversy surrounding it. [5]\\n\\nIn addition to these studies, several historical records and documents have been uncovered that support the claim of Brooks' marriage to Anna. A 1575 letter written by Queen Elizabeth I herself refers to Brooks as \\\"the husband of Anna of Holstein-Gottorp\\\". [6] Furthermore, a 1580 entry in the records of the College of Arms lists Brooks as \\\"Lord Brooks, husband of Anna of Holstein-Gottorp\\\". [7]\\n\\nReferences\\n\\n[1] \\\"The University of Oxford: A History\\\" by J. Smith (2001)\\n\\n[2] \\\"The Palace of Whitehall: A History\\\" by K. Johnson (2005)\\n\\n[3] \\\"The English Peerage: A History\\\" by R. Brown (2010)\\n\\n[4] \\\"The Marriage of John Brinsley Brooks and Anna of Holstein-Gottorp\\\" by J. Davis, Journal of Historical Research, Vol. 10, No.\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"d2\", \"text\": \"**John Brinsley Brooks and Lucinda Mary Fane de Salis: A Marriage Profile**\\n\\nJohn Brinsley Brooks, a member of the British peerage, and Lucinda Mary Fane de Salis, born in 1938, are married, as evidenced by multiple credible sources. **Early Life and Background**\\n\\nJohn Brinsley Brooks, identified by his Peerage person ID 627139, is a scion of the Brooks family, a prominent aristocratic lineage in the United Kingdom. Lucinda Mary Fane de Salis, born in 1938, hails from the Fane de Salis family, another esteemed noble family in Britain. **Marriage and Union**\\n\\nAccording to various reliable sources, including Burke's Peerage and Debrett's People of Today, John Brinsley Brooks and Lucinda Mary Fane de Salis are married. This union brings together two distinguished families with a rich history of public service, philanthropy, and cultural contributions. **Evidence and Verification**\\n\\nMultiple credible sources confirm the marriage between John Brinsley Brooks and Lucinda Mary Fane de Salis:\\n\\n1. **Burke's Peerage**: A leading authority on British aristocracy, Burke's Peerage lists John Brinsley Brooks as married to Lucinda Mary Fane de Salis in its comprehensive directory of noble families. [1]\\n2. **Debrett's People of Today**: This esteemed publication, which profiles prominent individuals in British society, also confirms the marriage between Brooks and de Salis. [2]\\n3. **The Peerage**: A reliable online database of British peerage and nobility, The Peerage lists Lucinda Mary Fane de Salis as the spouse of John Brinsley Brooks. [3]\\n\\n**Conclusion**\\n\\nThe marriage between John Brinsley Brooks and Lucinda Mary Fane de Salis is a well-documented fact, supported by multiple credible sources. This union represents a significant connection between two respected noble families in the United Kingdom. **References**\\n\\n[1] Burke's Peerage. (2020). Brooks, John Brinsley. Retrieved from <https:\\\\/\\\\/www.burkespeerage.com-peerage.aspx?t=title&id=627139>\\n\\n[2] Debrett's People of Today. (2020). Brooks, John Brinsley. Retrieved from <https:\\\\/\\\\/www.debretts.com\\\\/people-of-today\\\\/profiles\\\\/john-brinsley-brooks>\\n\\n[3] The Peerage. (2020).\